course: biology
lesson: 209
title: Cows: cud, milk, burps and the red cape myth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Grass is so tough that people cannot get any goodness from it at all, yet a cow turns it into milk and meat.
explain: Its secret is a stomach with four parts, the biggest of them a huge vat full of microbes, living things far too tiny to see, that break the grass down.
explain: That is why a cow lying in a field seems to chew and chew with nothing new going in: it is bringing swallowed grass back up and chewing it a second time, which is called chewing the cud.
explain: Cows are also more social and clever than people think: they have best friends, learn routines and their names, and the bull charging the red cape is chasing the movement, not the colour.
fact: A cow chews the cud: it swallows grass, later brings it back up and chews it again to break it down, for many hours a day.
fact: Books sometimes say a cow has four stomachs, but it really has one stomach with four parts, and the biggest part is full of microbes that break down tough grass.
fact: A cow makes milk only after having a calf, because, like all mammals, mothers make milk for their babies; a dairy cow can give over twenty litres a day.
fact: Only female cattle give milk, from the udder, the bag under the tummy: «cow» means a female, males are bulls or bullocks, the young are calves and a group is a herd.
fact: The microbes in a cow's stomach make methane, a gas that warms the climate, and cows burp most of it out; scientists are testing seaweed in cow feed to cut it.
fact: The saying that cows lie down before rain is a myth: cows lie down to rest and chew the cud whatever the weather.
fact: Cattle cannot see red the way we do, and a bull charges a matador's cape (the cloth a bullfighter waves) because it moves, not because it is red.
fact: Cows in a field often face the same way, turning their backs to wind and rain or lining up with the sun; some scientists reported they tend to line up north to south, but others could not find this, so it is still argued.
fact: Cows form friendships, and studies found they are calmer with a best friend nearby; calves kept with their mothers learn better social skills.
fact: Cows learn routines and their names, and on farms with milking robots, cows walk in by themselves when they choose to be milked.
fact: A cow has no top front teeth, only a hard pad, so it wraps its long rough tongue around grass to pull it, and that tongue is long enough to lick inside its own nostrils.
exercise: What is a cow doing when it chews while lying down with nothing new to eat? | Chewing the cud, food brought back up from its stomach | Chewing gum it found in the field that someone dropped | Practising for its next meal | Grinding its teeth because it is angry with the farmer
exercise: Why does a cow chew its food twice? | To break tough grass down well | Because it forgot it already ate | To make the grass taste sweeter | To keep its teeth from falling out
exercise: How many stomachs does a cow really have? | One stomach with four parts | Four separate stomachs in a row | Two stomachs that work side by side | No stomach, just a long tube
exercise: What do the microbes in a cow's stomach do? | They break down tough grass | They make the cow's black spots | They help the cow see at night | They keep the cow warm in winter
exercise: When does a cow start making milk? | After it has had a calf | As soon as it is born | Only when it eats enough grass | Only when it is milked every day
exercise: About how much milk can a dairy cow give in a day? | Over twenty litres | About one small cup | Exactly one litre a day | Over a thousand litres
exercise: What is a male of the cattle family called? | A bull or a bullock | A cow | A heifer | A calf
exercise: What is a young cow called? | A calf | A foal | A kid | A lamb
exercise: What gas do cow burps release? | Methane, which warms the climate | Oxygen for us to breathe | Pure steam | Smoke from their stomach fire
exercise: What are scientists adding to cow food to cut methane? | Seaweed | Sugar lumps | Beach sand | Salt water only
exercise: Why do cows lie down? | To rest and chew the cud, whatever the weather | Because they know rain is coming and want a dry patch | Because their legs cannot hold them up for long | To hide from the farmer
exercise: What makes a bull charge at a matador's cape? | The cape moving | The bright red colour | The smell of the cloth | The music in the arena
exercise: Why might cows in a field all face the same way? | They turn their backs to wind and rain or line up with the sun | They are all watching the same television in the barn | The farmer ties them in lines | They always face the milking shed, waiting for the farmer to come
exercise: Do scientists agree that cows line up north to south? | No, some found it and others could not | Yes, every cow always does | Yes, cows carry tiny compasses | No, cows never stand still
exercise: What did studies find about cows and best friends? | Cows are calmer with a best friend nearby | Cows hate all other cows | Cows only make friends with dogs | Cows forget their friends after one day in a new field
exercise: How do cows on robot farms get milked? | They walk in by themselves when they choose | The robot catches them with a net | The farmer carries each one | They are milked only once a year
exercise: How does a cow pull up grass without top front teeth? | It wraps its long rough tongue around it | It pulls the grass up with its hooves | It sucks the grass up through its nose | It bites with its back teeth only
test: In the film Barnyard, the boy cows have udders and give milk. What is wrong? | Only females give milk; males are bulls and have no udders | Nothing, all cattle give milk, the boys and the girls alike | Only bulls give milk | Calves give the most milk
test: «bro, it says in my book cows have 4 stomachs lol» What is more exact? | One stomach with four parts | Four stomachs and four hearts | One stomach and no parts | Four stomachs, one in each leg
test: «3 cows in the field, how many stomach parts altogether» | 12 | 3 | 4 | 9
test: «2 cows, a bull and 2 calves, how many legs. oh and the farmer's dog» | 24 | 20 | 22 | 26
test: «cows lying down, gonna rain, cancel the picnic» What is right? | Cows lie down to rest whatever the weather, so it tells you nothing | Yes, cows always know when rain is coming, they feel it in their legs | Yes, but only brown cows | No, cows lying down means snow
test: «paint the cape blue then and the bull won't charge, ha» What would really happen? | It would still charge, because it chases the movement | It would run away from blue | It would lie down and sleep | It would only charge at green, because bulls hate green grass that is too short
test: What would happen if a cow had no microbes in its stomach? | It could not break down grass properly | It would make more milk | It would grow wings | It would burp more methane
test: «why can't I just eat grass like a cow, it's free» What is right? | People have no microbe-filled stomach to break grass down | People can digest grass perfectly well if they chew it for long enough | Grass is poisonous to every animal | Only cows are allowed to eat grass
test: «cow's licking its own nose holes, gross, is it ill» What is right? | No, cows have long tongues and do it normally | Yes, it must see a vet at once | Yes, it is trying to stop a sneeze | No, it is tasting the coming rain on the wind, which cows always do before a storm
test: «that cow keeps following the other one everywhere, weird» What is the likely reason? | Cows have best friends and stay close to them | It wants to steal the best grass from it | It is lost and cannot see where it is going | It is the other cow's calf, always
test: «cows are thick, they just stand there» Which fact shows that is unfair? | Cows learn routines and their names and choose when to be milked | Cows can read the farm sign | Cows never move all day | Cows cannot tell one another apart, even the cows they live with every day
test: «why does milk cost so much, cows just make it all the time» What is right? | A cow makes milk only after having a calf | Cows make milk from the day they are born | Bulls make half the milk | Cows only make milk on Sundays
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the digestive apparatus of the domestic cow? | A single stomach divided into four compartments, the largest containing microbes which digest grass | Four entirely separate stomachs, each digesting a distinct food | A single undivided stomach incapable of digesting grass | No stomach whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal stimulus which provokes a bull to charge the cape of a matador. | The movement of the cape | The red colouration of the cape | The odour of the fabric | The sound of the crowd alone
exam: It is given that a herd of cattle is observed lying down in a pasture. Ascertain the conclusion which may properly be drawn. | The animals are resting and ruminating, and no forecast of the weather may be inferred | Precipitation is certain within the hour, the animals having sensed the approach of the rain | The animals are gravely ill | Snowfall is imminent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the production of milk by cattle. | Milk is produced by females following the birth of a calf | Milk is produced by bulls and cows alike | Milk is produced from birth irrespective of calving | Milk is produced solely by calves
